insert into public.academy_course_structures
    (app_slug, tier, structure, content_version)
insert into public.academy_quiz_questions
    (app_slug, tier, scope, module_key, ord, prompt, options, answer_index, explanation)
values
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-a-gas-line-is-not-a-liquid-line', 1, 'The SOKU trunk runs 850.000000 psia at the station against 620.000000 psia at the delivery point, and the first quantity any transmission form builds is 338100.0000 psia squared. Why does a gas form square the two pressures before subtracting them?', '["Squaring keeps the group positive whichever way round the two pressures are handed in, which is what lets one form serve a line read from either end without a separate sign convention for the delivery point.", "The square is a linearisation that holds while the pressure ratio stays near one, and it is replaced by the plain difference on any line whose ends are far apart.", "The density the friction acts on is itself proportional to the pressure, so the gas is heavier and slower where the line is full and lighter and faster where it has spent itself, and integrating that along the pipe puts the pressures inside a square.", "The gas is compressible, so the volume at the outlet is larger than the volume at the inlet, and squaring both pressures is how the form carries that ratio of volumes into a single term."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A liquid line subtracts pressures because the fluid leaving is the fluid that entered, at one density. A gas line cannot, and the whole compressible half of the course follows from that.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-a-gas-line-is-not-a-liquid-line', 2, 'A report quotes the SOKU driving group as a pressure of 338100.0000 psi. What is wrong with that line of the report?', '["The group is 338100.0000 psia squared, and its units say so, because it is the difference of two squared pressures rather than a pressure difference at all.", "Nothing is wrong with the units, because the group is formed from two absolute pressures and therefore carries the same units they do, but the figure should be quoted at six decimals as a pressure rather than at four.", "The group should be reported as the ordinary difference between the two ends in psi, with the squared form kept inside the calculation and never quoted on a report at all.", "The group is correct in psi but incomplete, because a driving group is only meaningful once the efficiency of 1.000000 has been applied to it."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The inlet squared less the outlet squared is 338100.0000 psia squared. Quoting a squared group as a pressure is the second of the two mistakes a liquid habit produces here.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-a-gas-line-is-not-a-liquid-line', 3, 'The module exports a base temperature of 520.000000 degR and a base pressure of 14.650000 psia. What does a rate of 66104956.1404 scfd actually assert?', '["That the line passes that volume each day at the conditions it actually flows at, which on this trunk are 535.000000 degR and a pressure somewhere between 850.000000 psia and 620.000000 psia.", "That the line passes that volume each day measured at the delivery point of 620.000000 psia, which is the condition a contract is written against and the reason the base is exported for a caller to read.", "That the line would pass that volume if it were run at 520.000000 degR and 14.650000 psia, which is why the base is a condition of the pipe rather than of the counting.", "That this quantity of gas, counted as the volume it would occupy at 520.000000 degR and 14.650000 psia, passes the line each day, whatever conditions it is actually at while it does so."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Nothing in the pipe is at the base. A standard cubic foot is an accounting unit, and the base is inside the form so the answer is a quantity rather than a volume at an unstated condition.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-a-gas-line-is-not-a-liquid-line', 4, 'Two low pressures appear in this engine: the base pressure of 14.650000 psia and atmospheric, which the engine names rather than numbering. What separates them?', '["The base is the value the four transmission forms were fitted at and atmospheric is the value the goldens were generated at, so the two belong to the published correlations and to this package''s own check of them.", "The base is a bookkeeping condition that sits inside every published form, and atmospheric is a physical floor that appears in the solver inverting them.", "They are two statements of the same condition at different precisions, which is why the engine exports one of them and leaves the other to be read off the first.", "The base applies to the gas half of the engine and atmospheric to the liquid half, so a single call never carries both."]'::jsonb, 1, 'One is where a standard cubic foot is counted and the other is where an outlet-pressure search stops. They hold different values and they do opposite jobs.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-a-gas-line-is-not-a-liquid-line', 5, 'Why can two gas rates counted at different bases not be compared, even in the case where the two numbers are equal?', '["Because each is a volume at its own stated condition, so an agreement between them is a coincidence of the arithmetic rather than a statement about how much gas either line passes.", "Because a difference of base changes the compressibility the form applies, and two rates computed at two compressibilities describe gas of two different gravities.", "Because the base enters each form linearly, so two rates at two bases differ by a fixed ratio and the equal case is the one in which that ratio has been applied twice.", "Because the base pressure sets the floor of the outlet-pressure bracket, so two rates counted at different bases were searched over different ranges."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Every rate in this tier is counted at 520.000000 degR and 14.650000 psia, which is exactly what lets the four forms be set beside each other honestly.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-a-gas-line-is-not-a-liquid-line', 6, 'Asked for the rate on the SOKU trunk with 900.000000 psia at the outlet against 850.000000 psia at the inlet, the engine answers with an error naming no flow. Why is that better than returning zero?', '["Zero would be dimensionally wrong, since the form returns a rate in scfd and a shut line has no rate at that base to report.", "Zero would be caught by the next check downstream anyway, so the refusal exists to save that check the work rather than to make a different statement.", "A rate of zero would be a claim that the line was considered and found to carry nothing, and it would be indistinguishable in a sweep from a line that is genuinely shut in.", "Zero would understate the line, because at 900.000000 psia against 850.000000 psia the gas flows backwards and the honest answer is a negative rate rather than an absent one."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The refusal says the question as asked has no answer in this method, because a form built on a driving group cannot be evaluated once the group has gone.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-a-gas-line-is-not-a-liquid-line', 7, 'The refusal on a dead line is worded to cover an outlet that meets or exceeds the inlet. What follows from the first of those two words?', '["An outlet standing exactly at the inlet is the boundary case the message admits, so the engine returns the smallest rate it can represent there and refuses only above it, which is what the wording is drawing attention to.", "An outlet within a narrow band of the inlet is refused as well, because a group that small cannot be resolved by the arithmetic and the engine declines the whole neighbourhood rather than a single point.", "An outlet exactly at the inlet is accepted and an outlet above it refused, which is the distinction between a group of zero and a group that has gone negative.", "An outlet standing exactly at the inlet is refused on the same terms as one standing above it."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The group is zero in the first case and negative in the second, and neither is a state the form can be evaluated at. There is no tolerance band and no nearly-equal case that quietly returns a very small rate.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-a-gas-line-is-not-a-liquid-line', 8, 'At an outlet of 845.000000 psia, five psi below the inlet, the trunk returns 10466013.4729 scfd. At 900.000000 psia it returns a refusal. What distinguishes the two?', '["At 845.000000 psia the line is inside the range the bisection searches and at 900.000000 psia it is above the ceiling of that bracket, so the second case is a search failure rather than a physical one.", "At 845.000000 psia the driving group is small and at 900.000000 psia it is absent, and a form built on that group can be evaluated in the first case and not in the second.", "At 845.000000 psia the engine falls back to a linearised form valid for small pressure differences, and at 900.000000 psia no such fallback exists.", "At 845.000000 psia the rate is small enough to be reported at the base of 14.650000 psia, and at 900.000000 psia it would have to be reported at a base the module does not export."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Five psi below the inlet is very nearly a dead line and it still has an answer. Small and absent are different states of the same trunk.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-a-gas-line-is-not-a-liquid-line', 9, 'The dead-line message arrives as an ordinary returned object carrying an error string rather than as a thrown exception. What is the cost of that choice?', '["A returned error cannot name the input that failed, so the caller has to reconstruct which of the eight conditions was at fault from the message text alone.", "A returned error is lost whenever the result is serialised, because an error string has no representation once the object is written out for a report.", "A studio that forgets to check the property does not crash, so an unchecked refusal flows onward as an object where a number was expected.", "A returned error forces every caller to wrap the call, which is the work a thrown exception would have done for them."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Nothing has to be wrapped to catch it, and that convenience cuts both ways. The check matters precisely because nothing compels it.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-a-gas-line-is-not-a-liquid-line', 10, 'The trunk is stated at an average flowing temperature of 535.000000 degR and an average compressibility of 0.885000. What does the word average commit the method to?', '["One temperature and one compressibility stand in for the whole 32.000000 miles, so the form has no way to represent a line that is hot where it leaves the station and cold where it arrives.", "That the engine averages the temperature along the line internally from the two end conditions, which is why only one figure is exposed to a caller.", "That the two figures are averages over the published cases in this package rather than over the length of any one line.", "That the temperature and the compressibility are averaged over the four transmission forms, each of which would otherwise use its own."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Choosing what to average over is part of setting a case up, and two engineers who average differently report different rates from the same pipe.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-a-gas-line-is-not-a-liquid-line', 11, 'Why is a gauge pressure handed to a gas form worse than a gauge pressure handed to a liquid one?', '["The gas forms report at a base pressure of 14.650000 psia, so a gauge pressure is already expressed against the wrong reference before the form reads it and the two errors compound.", "A liquid form subtracts the two pressures and the offset survives that subtraction, while a gas form divides them and an offset in a ratio grows without limit as the pressures approach one another.", "The gas forms take the flowing temperature in degR, so a caller who has supplied a gauge pressure has usually supplied a temperature on the wrong scale too.", "The error goes through a square, so it stops being a constant offset and no later step recovers it."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Every pressure in this half of the engine is absolute, which is why the trunk is quoted at 850.000000 psia and 620.000000 psia and the temperature at 535.000000 degR.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-a-gas-line-is-not-a-liquid-line', 12, 'Two trunks in different climates carry different gas. What does each of them report its rate against?', '["Its own flowing temperature and compressibility, which on SOKU are 535.000000 degR and 0.885000, since those are the conditions the gas is actually at when it passes the metering.", "The same 520.000000 degR and 14.650000 psia, because the base does not change with the line.", "The base scaled by its own compressibility, which is how a contract written in scfd stays comparable between two lines carrying gas of different gravities.", "Whichever of the four transmission forms it was computed with, each of which carries its own base as part of its published statement."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The flowing temperature and the compressibility belong to the line and change with it. The base does not, and that is what makes a contract written in scfd mean the same thing at both ends.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-a-gas-line-is-not-a-liquid-line', 13, 'A gathering scheme returns the no-flow refusal on one branch. What may not be concluded from it?', '["That this branch passes nothing on the conditions as given, which is the whole of what the message asserts.", "That the two pressures as handed in do not describe flow from the station towards the delivery point.", "That the bore on that branch is too small, because the message says nothing about the pipe.", "That no rate was computed for the branch at all, since a form built on a driving group is never evaluated once the group has gone."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The message says the pressures as given do not describe flow from the inlet to the outlet. A larger bore, a higher inlet and a lower target are separate questions.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-a-gas-line-is-not-a-liquid-line', 14, 'The same trunk reads 66104956.1404 scfd at an outlet of 620.000000 psia and 10466013.4729 scfd at an outlet of 845.000000 psia. Which quantity accounts for the whole difference?', '["The driving group, since the bore, the length, the gravity, the temperature, the compressibility and the efficiency are identical on both readings.", "The compressibility, which the engine re-averages at each outlet pressure and which falls as the two ends of the line approach one another.", "The efficiency, which is the only input in a transmission form able to move an answer by that proportion and the reason every rate is quoted with it.", "The base, since a rate counted at 14.650000 psia against one counted at the line''s own outlet differ by the ratio of those two pressures."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The pipe did not change and neither did the gas. Held at 620.000000 psia the group is 338100.0000 psia squared, and brought up to 845.000000 psia it is very much smaller.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-a-gas-line-is-not-a-liquid-line', 15, 'Of the eight conditions the SOKU trunk is stated at, which ones does a transmission form square?', '["The inlet of 850.000000 psia and the outlet of 620.000000 psia, and also the bore of 11.938000 in, since a gas form carries an area and an area is a squared length.", "The inlet of 850.000000 psia alone, the outlet entering the group linearly, which is what makes the group asymmetric between the two ends of a line.", "The two pressures and the average flowing temperature of 535.000000 degR, all three of which appear inside the driving group of 338100.0000 psia squared.", "The inlet of 850.000000 psia and the outlet of 620.000000 psia, and none of the other six conditions, no one of which is squared anywhere in a transmission form."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Eight conditions, and the first two never appear in a gas form on their own. They appear squared, as 338100.0000 psia squared.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-four-transmission-forms', 1, 'On the SOKU trunk the four forms read 66104956.1404, 86864172.0167, 88369202.2673 and 73861363.0502 scfd on identical inputs. What does that spread of 1.336801 establish?', '["That three of the four are being computed incorrectly, because one pipe under one set of conditions carries one rate and four answers cannot all be right about it.", "That the disagreement lies inside the published correlations, since the bore, the length, the two pressures, the gravity, the temperature, the compressibility and the efficiency were the same on all four calls.", "That the efficiency of 1.000000 is too high for this trunk, since a spread of that width closes once each form is run at the efficiency it was fitted at.", "That the forms disagree about the elevation term, which is the one part of a transmission form that is not shared between them."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Only the name of the form changed between the four rows. Whatever separates 66104956.1404 scfd from 88369202.2673 scfd is inside the correlations and nowhere else.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-four-transmission-forms', 2, 'Where do Weymouth and Panhandle B sit among the four forms on the SOKU trunk?', '["Weymouth is the lowest at 66104956.1404 scfd and Panhandle A the highest at 86864172.0167 scfd, with Panhandle B between Panhandle A and General Flow at 88369202.2673 scfd.", "Weymouth is the highest at 66104956.1404 scfd and Panhandle B the lowest at 88369202.2673 scfd, the ratio column of 1.336801 being read against the largest of the four rather than against Weymouth.", "General Flow is the lowest at 73861363.0502 scfd and Panhandle B the highest at 88369202.2673 scfd, Weymouth sitting above General Flow because it carries the largest diameter exponent of the four.", "Weymouth is the lowest at 66104956.1404 scfd and Panhandle B the highest at 88369202.2673 scfd."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Against Weymouth the column reads 1.000000, 1.314034, 1.336801 and 1.117335, so General Flow is second lowest and Panhandle B is the top of the four.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-four-transmission-forms', 3, 'General Flow returns 73861363.0502 scfd on the SOKU trunk together with a friction factor of 0.0112132010. What can be asked of that answer that cannot be asked of the other three?', '["What surface the rate was computed for, because the returned factor is the only statement any of the four forms makes about the roughness of the pipe.", "What rate the line would carry at a different efficiency, because the returned factor is what the efficiency multiplies and the other three apply it to the rate directly.", "Whether the iteration converged, because the factor is reported alongside a flag the other three have no iteration to produce.", "What the equivalent length factor was on the call, because a form that solves for its own friction has to report the length that friction acted over."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A Weymouth or a Panhandle answer carries no roughness at all, so it cannot be interrogated about the pipe it assumed. The factor is a result rather than an input.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-four-transmission-forms', 4, 'The diameter exponent of each form is reported as measured rather than quoted. How was the measurement taken?', '["The rate was differentiated against the bore across the published cases, whose bores run from 6.065000 in to 16.000000 in, and the exponent is the slope that fit produces.", "The leading constant of each published form was divided out of the SOKU rate, and what remains once the length and the pressures are removed is the diameter term.", "The form was asked for a rate at a bore of 10.000000 in and again at 20.000000 in, and the exponent is the base-two logarithm of the ratio of those two engine rates.", "Each form was run at the SOKU bore of 11.938000 in and the exponent read off the ratio of that rate to the rate the next form gives on the same bore."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Doubling makes the arithmetic legible, because the ratio across a factor of two in diameter is two raised to the exponent. The constants in front of each form divide out of a ratio.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-four-transmission-forms', 5, 'Which form gains most from a larger bore and which gains least?', '["Panhandle B gains most at 2.5300000000 and Weymouth least at 2.6666666667, since a form that already returns the highest rate on a given trunk has the most room to grow when the pipe is enlarged.", "Weymouth gains most at 2.6666666667 and Panhandle B least at 2.5300000000.", "Panhandle A gains most at 2.6182000000 and General Flow least at 2.5974750199, the two Panhandles bracketing the other pair on this table.", "General Flow gains most at 2.5974750199, because it is the only form that resolves its own friction factor and therefore the only one whose response to a bore change is computed rather than fitted."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The exponent is what says how much a bigger pipe buys. Weymouth carries the largest of the four and Panhandle B the smallest.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-four-transmission-forms', 6, 'Panhandle B returns the highest rate of the four on the SOKU trunk and carries the smallest diameter exponent of the four. Why is that not a contradiction?', '["The exponent is measured at bores of 10.000000 in and 20.000000 in while the rate is quoted at 11.938000 in, and a form''s exponent is only defined over the range of bores it was measured across.", "The exponent describes how the rate responds to length rather than to bore, so a form can carry the smallest exponent of the four and still return the largest rate on a trunk of 32.000000 miles.", "The efficiency of 1.000000 on the SOKU call suppresses the diameter term, so the ordering of the rates on that trunk reflects the leading constants of the four forms alone.", "The rate a form gives on one line and the way it responds to a change of bore are separate properties of the correlation."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Panhandle B reads 88369202.2673 scfd on this trunk and carries 2.5300000000. One is where the form starts and the other is how fast it climbs.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-four-transmission-forms', 7, 'At a bore of 10.000000 in Panhandle B reads 56450013.6000 scfd against Panhandle A''s 54628394.3526 scfd. At 20.000000 in the two read 326039290.8986 and 335409354.2407 scfd. What does that pair of rows show?', '["That which of the two Panhandles is higher is a question about the bore rather than a fact about the forms, because the higher exponent overtakes the higher starting point somewhere between those two sizes.", "That Panhandle B loses accuracy above 10.000000 in, which is why its published cases in this package stop at a bore of 16.000000 in and the crossing is outside the range either form was fitted for.", "That the two forms are the same correlation stated at two bores, the apparent crossing being the point at which the published constant of one is replaced by the constant of the other.", "That the exponents were measured on the wrong pair of bores, since an ordering that reverses across the measurement interval cannot be used to derive a single exponent for either form."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Panhandle A carries 2.6182000000 against Panhandle B''s 2.5300000000. An ordering established on one bore is an ordering on that bore, and this pair crosses inside the range an ordinary trunk is built at.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-four-transmission-forms', 8, 'General Flow at an efficiency of 0.850000 reads 62616519.4059 scfd and at 0.900000 reads 66364215.5127 scfd. Weymouth at an efficiency of 1.000000 reads 66104956.1404 scfd. What do those three figures show together?', '["That the efficiency is the dominant input of the two forms, since moving General Flow from 0.850000 to 0.900000 shifts it further than the whole gap between the two correlations at equal efficiency.", "That General Flow and Weymouth agree at an efficiency somewhere near 0.900000, which is the efficiency at which the two published correlations were fitted to the same data.", "That a form standing above another at equal efficiency can fall below it at a lower one, since General Flow is under the Weymouth figure at 0.850000 and over it at 0.900000.", "That an efficiency below 0.900000 is outside the range General Flow is valid over, which is why its rate there falls under a Weymouth rate computed at full efficiency."]'::jsonb, 2, 'General Flow reads 73861363.0502 scfd against Weymouth''s 66104956.1404 scfd at equal efficiency. Two forms at two efficiencies can be ordered any way the efficiencies please, so the efficiency travels with the rate.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-four-transmission-forms', 9, 'The efficiency is held for the literature in this course. What does that status require of a graded gas figure?', '["That it is computed at an efficiency of 1.000000, which is the only value the course will grade against and the reason the published cases at 0.950000 and 0.920000 are taught rather than examined.", "That it states the efficiency it was computed at, because every form carries it and no publication in this package stands behind any particular value of it.", "That it is quoted as a ratio against Weymouth rather than as a rate, since a ratio between two forms at one efficiency is independent of what that efficiency was.", "That it carries the friction factor the form settled on, which records what the pipe was assumed to be when no efficiency can be defended."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Weymouth moves from 56189212.7194 scfd at 0.850000 to 66104956.1404 scfd at 1.000000 on one pipe. A rate quoted without its efficiency is not a reproducible number.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-four-transmission-forms', 10, 'Every published case in this package prints the engine rate and the golden beside it. What does agreement on all four forms prove?', '["That the four forms have been reconciled against one another, since a golden set generated from a single source cannot carry four different answers for one pipe without an inconsistency showing up.", "That the spread of 1.336801 between the forms is inside the tolerance the goldens are checked at, which is what allows any of the four to be quoted for this trunk.", "That the disagreement between the forms is an artefact of the engine rather than of the correlations, because a golden agreement removes the implementation as a source of it.", "That each form is being computed as published, and nothing about which of them is right."]'::jsonb, 3, 'An implementation problem is one form drifting away from its own golden. A method disagreement is every form sitting on its own golden and the four still returning different rates.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-four-transmission-forms', 11, 'The published 8.000000 in case at 25.000000 miles is run twice through Weymouth on otherwise identical inputs, at 800.000000 ft of rise and 800.000000 ft of fall. It reads 35264696.4591 scfd and 36855536.7412 scfd. Which is which, and why is the pair in the set?', '["Climbing gives 35264696.4591 scfd and descending 36855536.7412 scfd, and the pair isolates the elevation term because every other condition on those two rows is identical to the last digit.", "Climbing gives 36855536.7412 scfd and descending 35264696.4591 scfd, a climbing line arriving with more pressure in hand because the column of gas it lifted is returned at the delivery point.", "The higher figure is the descending case, and the pair is in the set to show that the elevation term is symmetric, since equal distances up and down move the rate by equal amounts.", "The two are the same case computed at two efficiencies, since the published set holds the elevation at 0.000000 ft for every Weymouth row and varies the efficiency instead."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The set moves one thing at a time, which is what lets a reader separate a bore error from a length error from an elevation error. A descent carries more.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-four-transmission-forms', 12, 'A designer runs all four forms and reports their average. What is wrong with that?', '["The average is weighted wrongly, because General Flow resolves its own friction factor and therefore deserves more weight than the three forms that do not.", "The average is defensible only once each form has been run at its own fitted efficiency, and on this trunk all four were run at 1.000000.", "The average is a fifth number with no publication behind it, no case it was ever fitted to, and no golden anywhere in this package that it could be checked against.", "The average is a reasonable central estimate but it discards the friction factor of 0.0112132010, which is the only figure on the four answers that can be checked."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Choosing is engineering rather than arithmetic. It is settled by what the line resembles, what the operator has calibrated against, and what a contract names.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-four-transmission-forms', 13, 'What position does General Flow occupy among the four forms on the SOKU trunk?', '["Second highest at 1.117335 against Weymouth, standing between the two Panhandles because its measured exponent of 2.5974750199 falls between theirs.", "Second lowest at 1.117335 against Weymouth, and closer to Weymouth than to either Panhandle, so the four forms do not sit evenly spaced across the spread between them.", "Lowest of the four at 1.117335 against Weymouth, since the iteration it runs converges from below and settles under every form that is evaluated in a single pass.", "Highest of the four at 1.117335 against Weymouth, the ratio column being read against Panhandle B as the reference rather than against Weymouth."]'::jsonb, 1, 'General Flow sits nearer the bottom of the four than the top. Against Weymouth it reads 1.117335 while Panhandle A reads 1.314034 and Panhandle B reads 1.336801.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-four-transmission-forms', 14, 'A reviewer takes the exponent of 2.6666666667 and uses it to scale a Panhandle B answer to a larger bore. What has gone wrong?', '["An exponent belongs to the correlation that carries it, and on this table the spread across the four is wide enough to reverse an ordering between two of them.", "Nothing has gone wrong arithmetically, but the result should be quoted against Weymouth rather than against Panhandle B, since 2.6666666667 is the Weymouth exponent and the ratio column is indexed on that form.", "The exponent was measured by doubling from 10.000000 in to 20.000000 in, so it may only be applied to a bore change of exactly that factor and not to an arbitrary one.", "An exponent describes the pipe rather than the form, so the error is in applying a figure measured on one trunk to a bore the trunk does not have."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Panhandle B carries 2.5300000000 and Weymouth 2.6666666667. An exponent is a property of a correlation and never of a pipe.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-four-transmission-forms', 15, 'One published case runs 16.000000 in over 80.000000 miles from 700.000000 psia to 650.000000 psia at an efficiency of 0.920000. What makes it a useful member of the set?', '["It is the only case in the set carrying an elevation change, which is what lets the shared elevation term be separated from the four forms'' differing friction treatments across a single row.", "It is the only case run at an efficiency other than 1.000000, so it is where the linear multiplier can be checked against a form''s own golden without a second variable moving.", "It is the case whose engine value and golden agree most closely across all four forms, which is why the set is anchored on it and the shorter cases read against it.", "It is the longest and widest case, and its two pressures are the closest together of any in the set, so a small driving group and a large pipe are read together on one row of the set."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Bores in the set run from 6.065000 in to 16.000000 in and lengths from 10.000000 to 80.000000 miles, so a reader can separate a bore error from a length error.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-the-elevation-adjustment', 1, 'On the SOKU trunk 1500.000000 ft of rise gives s as 0.0795976556 and 1500.000000 ft of fall gives -0.0795976556, while 3000.000000 ft of fall gives -0.1591953113. What property of s do those three rows establish?', '["That s is proportional to the elevation change only over falls, the rise column being the mirror image the engine writes rather than a value it computes separately.", "That s grows faster than the elevation change, which is the asymmetry between climbing and descending that the rest of the module is about.", "That s is proportional to the elevation change, so equal distances up and down give exactly opposite values and doubling the fall doubles s.", "That s is bounded between -1.0000000000 and 1.0000000000, which is what allows it to be handed to an exponential without the group overflowing on a tall hill."]'::jsonb, 2, 's itself is well behaved. Everything awkward about a hill happens after s, when it goes into an exponential.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-the-elevation-adjustment', 2, 'At 1500.000000 ft of rise e to the s is 1.0828513009 and at 1500.000000 ft of fall it is 0.9234878318. What does each of those do to the driving group?', '["The rise counts the outlet''s square for more than it is, which shrinks the group and the rate, and the fall counts it for less, which enlarges both the group and the rate.", "The rise counts the inlet''s square for more, which enlarges the group, and the fall counts the inlet''s square for less, the multiplier sitting on the upstream end because that is where the column of gas starts.", "The rise scales both squares together, so the group moves by that factor as a whole while the ratio between the two ends is preserved, which is why a flat test case cannot detect the term.", "The rise is subtracted from the group as a static head in psia squared, and the fall is added to it, in the same way a liquid line adds and subtracts an elevation head."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The group becomes the inlet squared less e to the s times the outlet squared. The inlet is untouched, and the whole of the hill is a single multiplier on the outlet.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-the-elevation-adjustment', 3, 'A reader applies e to the s to both pressures in the group rather than to the outlet alone. Why does that error survive testing so well?', '["Scaling both ends leaves the group unchanged, because the multiplier cancels between the two squared pressures and the error has no effect on any line at all until an elevation change of 3000.000000 ft or more.", "Scaling both ends produces a group that is still zero when the pressures meet, and since the engine tests the dead-line refusal at every call the error is caught only on a line whose outlet meets its inlet.", "Scaling both ends reproduces the equivalent length factor of 1.0408761444 as a side effect, so the two halves of the adjustment appear to agree and the case passes its own internal check.", "Scaling both ends leaves the group proportional to the flat one, so every rate is wrong by the same factor and a sweep stays self-consistent."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Nothing inside a single case contradicts anything else in it. The error also vanishes on a flat line, where e to the s is exactly 1.0000000000.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-the-elevation-adjustment', 4, 'The elevation group returns a second column beside e to the s. At 1500.000000 ft of rise it reads 1.0408761444 and at 3000.000000 ft of fall it reads 0.9244633111. What does that column act on?', '["The outlet pressure, as a second multiplier applied after e to the s, the two together forming the full adjustment the group carries.", "The length the friction acts over, so a climb behaves as though the pipe were longer than it was laid and a descent as though it were shorter.", "The gas gravity of 0.670000, which is scaled to an effective value for the column of gas standing in the inclined section of the line.", "The compressibility of 0.885000, which the engine corrects for the pressure distribution a hill produces along the line."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Two columns, two jobs. The first scales a pressure inside the group and the second scales a length outside it. A factor of 1.0408761444 says the friction behaves as though the climb were over four percent more pipe.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-the-elevation-adjustment', 5, 'At an elevation change of 0.000000 ft both e to the s and the equivalent length factor read exactly 1.0000000000. Why is that the cleanest check on an elevation implementation?', '["Because a flat line is the only case in which the two columns can be compared against each other, the rest of the table having no independent value to check either column against.", "Because the flat case is the only one whose rate is published with a golden, so an implementation that returns the flat form exactly has been checked against the one figure in the table that was measured.", "Because the two parts of the adjustment vanish at the same moment and the flat form comes back unchanged, so a term that is merely near one on a flat line is a term that is wrong everywhere.", "Because both columns are exponentials of s and an exponential of zero is one, so the check confirms that s was computed in the right units before anything else is examined."]'::jsonb, 2, 'At an elevation change of 0.000000 ft, s is 0.0000000000 and the adjustment collapses. An elevation term leaving a residue at zero is wrong on every flat line it will be given.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-the-elevation-adjustment', 6, 'The coefficient inside s is reported as 0.037500000000, and the digest says it was measured rather than typed. How was it obtained?', '["The engine was asked for s at a gravity of one, a rise of 1000.000000 ft, an absolute temperature of one and a compressibility of one, and the 37.500000 it returned was divided by the thousand feet.", "The engine was asked for s at the SOKU conditions and at 1500.000000 ft of rise, and 0.0795976556 was divided by the gravity, the temperature and the compressibility in turn until only the elevation remained.", "The coefficient was read off the ratio of the two equivalent length factors at 1500.000000 ft of rise and 1500.000000 ft of fall, which isolates it from everything else in the group.", "The coefficient was recovered from the base conditions of 520.000000 degR and 14.650000 psia, which is where the field units of the published forms enter the elevation term."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Setting three of the four inputs to one leaves the coefficient and the elevation change, so dividing by the elevation change leaves the coefficient. It is an engine return rather than a figure copied beside one.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-the-elevation-adjustment', 7, 'Which quantities is s built from?', '["The gas gravity, the elevation change, the absolute flowing temperature and the length of the line in miles, the length entering because the hill is spread along it.", "The gas gravity, the elevation change and the two pressures, since the column of gas standing in the pipe weighs more where the line is at its inlet pressure than where it is at its outlet.", "The gas gravity, the elevation change, the compressibility and the transmission efficiency, the efficiency entering here because it is the one multiplier shared by all four forms.", "The gas gravity, the elevation change, the absolute flowing temperature and the compressibility."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Those four are what decide how much a column of this gas weighs, which is what the term is for. A heavier gas, a taller hill, a colder line or a lower compressibility all make the column count for more.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-the-elevation-adjustment', 8, 's is arranged to carry no units. Why does that matter?', '["Because it is compared against the equivalent length factor, and two quantities can only be compared when neither carries a unit the other does not.", "Because it is handed to an exponential, and a quantity about to be exponentiated that still carries feet or degrees has gone wrong upstream.", "Because it is reported to ten decimal places, a precision that would be meaningless on a quantity carrying a physical unit.", "Because it is shared across all four forms, and the four are stated in different unit systems which only a dimensionless group can travel between."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The coefficient of 0.037500000000 belongs to the field units these forms are written in and would be a different number in any other system.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-the-elevation-adjustment', 9, 'At 1500.000000 ft of rise the four forms fall to 0.932862, 0.927767, 0.931566 and 0.931850 of their flat rates. Which form is most sensitive to this hill?', '["Weymouth, which falls to 0.932862, because it carries the largest diameter exponent of the four and a form more sensitive to the bore is more sensitive to the profile.", "Panhandle B, which falls to 0.931566, since it returns the largest rate on the flat trunk and therefore has the most rate available to lose.", "Panhandle A, which falls furthest at 0.927767 on the climb and also gains most on the descent at 1.068566, so it is the most sensitive of the four in both directions.", "General Flow, which falls to 0.931850, because it is the only form that resolves its friction factor against its own rate and so responds to the hill twice."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Panhandle A is the most sensitive in both directions and Weymouth the least, losing least at 0.932862 and gaining least at 1.063402.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-the-elevation-adjustment', 10, 'Averaging the Weymouth up and down fractions of 0.932862 and 1.063402 gives 0.998132. What is the point of printing that figure?', '["That it is close to one and is not one, so a route that climbs 1500.000000 ft and then descends the same distance has not in fact done nothing to the gas.", "That it is close to one, so an undulating profile may be netted to its total elevation change with an error small enough to ignore on any ordinary route.", "That it is below one, which is the correction the engine returns on every call carrying an elevation change and applies to the rate before reporting it.", "That it is the correction a designer applies to a flat-line rate before quoting it for a route with relief on it, which is why it is computed from the Weymouth row."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The term is an exponential and an exponential is not symmetric about zero. On that row the climb loses more than the descent gains, which is why the average falls below one rather than above it.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-the-elevation-adjustment', 11, 'All four forms share one elevation term and the four still respond to the same hill by different amounts. Why?', '["Each form receives the elevation change in its own units, the two Panhandles taking it in feet and Weymouth and General Flow scaling it by the length in miles before the group is formed.", "Each form computes its own s, because s carries the compressibility and each of the four applies a different average compressibility to the line it is describing.", "Each form applies the equivalent length factor and only General Flow applies e to the s as well, which is why its response to a hill differs from the other three.", "Each form combines the shared adjustment with its own friction treatment."]'::jsonb, 3, 'They disagree about friction and they agree about gravity. So a project that has chosen its form has also chosen how sensitive its answer is to the survey.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-the-elevation-adjustment', 12, 'What is the practical consequence of the elevation term sitting inside an exponential rather than being added as a head?', '["A tall enough hill drives the term to zero and the rate with it, which is the refusal a climbing line returns when its inlet cannot pay for the column.", "The climb costs more than the matching descent gives back, so the ups and downs of a profile do not cancel in the way that a liquid line''s do.", "The descent gives back more than the matching climb costs, so a route with relief on it carries more gas than the same route laid flat.", "The term cannot be separated from the friction, so a rate on a hill carries no decomposition into what the slope cost and what the pipe cost."]'::jsonb, 1, 'On the 1500.000000 ft table the Weymouth row reads 0.932862 climbing against 1.063402 descending, so the climb loses more than the descent gains and the two average to 0.998132 rather than to one.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-the-elevation-adjustment', 13, 'A designer nets an undulating route to a single elevation change and hands that figure to a gas form. What does the engine do?', '["It accepts the net figure and answers it honestly, and the answer it gives is for a line that climbs once rather than for a line that undulates.", "It refuses, because the elevation guard compares the rise against the length of the line and a netted profile cannot satisfy that comparison.", "It returns the rate together with the equivalent length factor of the netted hill, which is the engine''s way of signalling that the profile was reduced before it arrived.", "It applies the net change to the driving group and the full climbing distance to the equivalent length factor, which recovers most of what the netting discarded."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Nothing in the call describes the shape of the ground between the two ends. Treating an undulating profile as though the ups and downs cancel is an assumption the arithmetic refuses.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-the-elevation-adjustment', 14, 'Which part of a transmission form would still be there at no flow at all?', '["The equivalent length factor, since a length is a property of the line rather than of the flow through it and is therefore the one term independent of the rate.", "The efficiency, which multiplies the finished rate and is therefore the only input whose effect does not depend on the gas moving.", "The elevation term, because it is the weight of the gas standing in the pipe, which is a quantity that column has whether or not anything at all is passing along the line.", "The driving group, since two pressures can be read off a shut line and squared without any gas passing between them."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The rest of a transmission form is about motion, and that includes the length the friction acts over, which multiplies a friction that has gone. A column of gas weighs what it weighs whether or not anything is moving.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-the-elevation-adjustment', 15, 'A result is being reproduced by hand, and the multiplier is taken from the engine while the length factor is taken from a textbook. What is wrong with that?', '["The textbook states the length factor against the physical length while the engine states it against the equivalent length, so the two differ by the factor itself and the error compounds.", "The engine''s multiplier is computed at the SOKU compressibility of 0.885000 while a textbook factor assumes a compressibility of one, so the two can only be combined once the second has been rescaled.", "The length factor is the one part of the adjustment the engine does not return, which is why it has to be taken from a publication and reconciled against the multiplier afterwards.", "Both columns are consequences of the same s, so mixing two sources for one physical quantity gives two figures that will not correspond."]'::jsonb, 3, 'There are not two elevation inputs and there is no way to have one without the other. They correspond only when every input behind s matches.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-the-outlet-pressure', 1, 'The studio knows the rate a terminal has contracted for and wants the pressure it will see. How does the engine answer a question it has no closed inversion for?', '["It rearranges whichever of the four forms was named into an explicit expression for the outlet, which is possible for all four because the driving group is quadratic in the outlet pressure.", "It bisects on the outlet pressure, calling the same published form the forward direction uses at every step, so no second implementation of the physics exists to drift away from the first.", "It runs the forward form across a sweep of outlet pressures and interpolates between the two rows that bracket the target rate, which is why the answer is reported to six decimals rather than exactly.", "It inverts Weymouth in closed form and corrects that answer for whichever form the caller named, using the ratio column of 1.000000, 1.314034, 1.336801 and 1.117335."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The form being inverted is the same code the forward direction uses. The search runs inside a bracket, and that bracket is a piece of physics rather than a convenience.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-the-outlet-pressure', 2, 'Each of the four forms was run forward from an outlet of 620.000000 psia to a rate, and each rate was handed back to the solver. All four recover 620.000000 psia with a difference of 0.000000 psi. What has been proved?', '["That the four forms agree with one another once the question is asked in the inverse direction, which is what makes a contracted outlet a safer quantity to work in than a rate.", "That the published forms are correct, since a correlation that returns the pressure it was given is reproducing the physics of the line in both directions.", "That the bisection converged, which is the flag the solver cannot otherwise report and the reason the round trip is run on every call before the answer is released.", "That the inversion is faithful to the form it inverts, to the precision printed, and nothing whatever about whether that form describes this line correctly."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The same correlation was used in both directions, so a round trip is a test of a solver and never a test of physics. It proves nothing about whether the form is right.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-the-outlet-pressure', 3, 'Asked for 60000000.0000 scfd through Weymouth, the flat trunk arrives at 666.307057 psia for a drop of 183.692943 psi. What kind of number is 666.307057 psia?', '["The endpoint of a search, which carries a different trust from a closed-form result because it is only as good as the bracket it searched and the form it called.", "A closed-form result, since the Weymouth relation can be solved for the outlet directly once the rate is known and only the other three forms require a search.", "An interpolated value, taken between the two nearest rows of the published Weymouth cases and therefore carrying the uncertainty of that table rather than of a solver.", "A measured value, since the 60000000.0000 scfd duty is one of the published cases this package carries a golden for."]'::jsonb, 0, 'No equation produced it. A closed-form answer can be wrong in its algebra, and a bracketed answer can be wrong by never having contained the answer at all.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-the-outlet-pressure', 4, 'The bracket the solver searches runs from atmospheric up to a ceiling. What is that ceiling?', '["The inlet pressure, since an outlet can never stand above the pressure the gas entered at and the search would otherwise waste itself on impossible values.", "The pressure at which the rate reaches the largest value the line can pass, which is the other end of the same interval whose floor is the largest rate at atmospheric outlet.", "The pressure at which the driving group vanishes, which is the inlet divided by the square root of e to the s, and which is not the inlet itself on any sloping line.", "The inlet multiplied by the square root of e to the s, so that a climb raises the ceiling to cover the extra head the column of gas demands."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Each ceiling on the trunk''s table is its own inlet of 850.000000 psia divided by the square root of the engine''s e to the s on that row. A hill moves it.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-the-outlet-pressure', 5, 'On the flat SOKU trunk the ceiling is 850.000000 psia, which is the inlet. Why is that case misleading?', '["Because the flat case is the only one the engine computes the ceiling for, the sloping rows being extrapolated from it by the equivalent length factor.", "Because e to the s is exactly 1.0000000000 and the square root of one changes nothing, so the flat line is the only case in which the intuition that a ceiling is an inlet is right.", "Because the flat case puts the ceiling at the inlet only to the precision printed, and a finer computation would show it fractionally below 850.000000 psia on every line.", "Because a flat line is the only profile on which the outlet can legitimately equal the inlet, which is the state the no-flow refusal covers."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A descent ends the bracket above the inlet and a climb ends it below. One case out of three puts the ceiling at the inlet, and it is the case the intuition was formed on.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-the-outlet-pressure', 6, 'Take the SOKU trunk up 3000.000000 ft. Where does its bracket end?', '["At 850.000000 psia, since a climbing line still enters at its inlet and the hill is expressed through the equivalent length factor rather than through the bracket.", "At 920.423606 psia, the climb raising the ceiling above the inlet because the gas must arrive with enough pressure in hand to have lifted itself 3000.000000 ft.", "At 784.964657 psia, which is 65.035343 psi below the 850.000000 psia the gas entered at, because the column of gas costs head that no rate at all gets back.", "At 766.523922 psia, which is the outlet the trunk delivers at on that hill and therefore the highest pressure the search has any reason to consider."]'::jsonb, 2, 'On that row e to the s is 1.1725669398, and 850.000000 psia divided by the square root of it is 784.964657 psia. The ceiling falls below the inlet on every climb.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-the-outlet-pressure', 7, 'A delivery point is quoted above the station pressure it is fed from, on a line that falls 3000.000000 ft. Is the figure admissible?', '["Yes, up to 920.423606 psia, which stands 70.423606 psi above the inlet, so a delivery point above a station is a real operating point rather than nonsense.", "No, because the inlet is the most pressure the line ever holds and a descent recovers its head along the pipe rather than at the delivery point.", "No, because the descent lowers the ceiling to 784.964657 psia, the gas arriving having spent both the friction and the column it ran down.", "Yes, but only up to 884.511201 psia, which is the ceiling for a descent and the pressure a delivery point sees once the static column has been returned to it."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The column of gas recovers more head than the friction spends. 884.511201 psia is the ceiling for a fall of 1500.000000 ft, and 920.423606 psia for a fall of twice that.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-the-outlet-pressure', 8, 'Equal hills of 3000.000000 ft put the ceiling 65.035343 psi below the inlet climbing and 70.423606 psi above it descending. At 1500.000000 ft the two figures are 33.164669 and 34.511201. What do those pairs show?', '["That the ceiling is asymmetric only on the larger hill, the 1500.000000 ft pair of 33.164669 and 34.511201 being equal to the precision the table is printed at and the asymmetry appearing between there and 3000.000000 ft.", "That the descent figures are the larger because they are measured from the ceiling down to the inlet while the climb figures are measured from the inlet down to the ceiling, so the two columns are reckoned from opposite ends.", "That the ceiling moves with the square of the elevation change, since doubling the hill from 1500.000000 ft to 3000.000000 ft roughly doubles both offsets rather than leaving them unchanged.", "That the ceiling inherits the asymmetry of the exponential, so equal slopes up and down do not move it by equal amounts."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Each ceiling is the inlet over the square root of e to the s, and e to the s is not symmetric about zero. The descent offset is the larger of the pair on both hills.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-the-outlet-pressure', 9, 'A bracket that runs from atmospheric to the inlet is used on a descent and then on a climb. How does it fail in each case?', '["On the descent it is too long, since the real ceiling sits below the inlet there, and on the climb it is too short, since the real ceiling sits above it.", "On the descent it is too short and cannot reach an answer lying above the inlet, and on the climb it is too long and reaches past the pressure where the group has already vanished.", "On the descent it fails to converge and reports so, and on the climb it converges on the floor, so only the descending case produces a visible symptom.", "On both it returns the inlet with a drop of 0.000000 psi, the search collapsing to its upper bound whenever the answer lies outside the interval."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The two failures are opposite and both return numbers. On a climb the band from 784.964657 psia up to 850.000000 psia has no physical content, because there is no rate to be found above the ceiling.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-the-outlet-pressure', 10, 'Run the SOKU trunk 3000.000000 ft downhill with its outlet at 880.000000 psia and it carries 29457958.5967 scfd, the outlet standing 30.000000 psia above the inlet. Hand the solver that rate and what comes back?', '["An outlet of 880.000000 psia and a drop of 30.000000 psi, the engine reporting the magnitude and leaving the direction to be read off the profile.", "A refusal, because the target outlet lies above the inlet and the no-flow message covers any outlet that meets or exceeds it.", "An outlet of 880.000000 psia and a drop of -30.000000 psi, the forward and inverse calls differing by 0.000000 psi.", "An outlet of 850.000000 psia and a drop of 0.000000 psi, the search returning its upper bound when the answer it is looking for sits above the inlet."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A negative drop is the honest reading of a descent and not a sign convention to be tidied up. The line arrives higher than it left because the column gave back more than the friction spent.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-the-outlet-pressure', 11, 'The same descent is run again with its outlet genuinely below the inlet, at 700.000000 psia, where it carries 65259103.7761 scfd and the solve recovers 700.000000 psia with an error of 0.000000 psi. Why does that second row matter?', '["It is the control: both rows use the same hill, the same trunk and the same bisection, so a fault that appears in one and not the other cannot be blamed on the search.", "It is the calibration row, since a case whose answer sits below the inlet is the only one a bracket of any width can be relied on to contain, and the first row is scaled against it.", "It shows that the solver is more accurate at lower outlet pressures, which is why a descending line should be worked from an outlet below the inlet wherever the profile allows it.", "It establishes the floor of the bracket, because 700.000000 psia is the lowest outlet this trunk will deliver at on that hill."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A weakness in the search itself would show on both rows at once, so a difference between them points at the range the search was given rather than at the search.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-the-outlet-pressure', 12, 'Asked for 20000000.0000 scfd up the 3000.000000 ft hill, the trunk delivers at 766.523922 psia for a drop of 83.476078 psi. What does that figure sit beside?', '["The ceiling of 850.000000 psia, which is the inlet the gas entered at and the pressure the outlet would approach if the rate were reduced towards nothing on any profile.", "The floor of the bracket, since a climb of that height leaves the delivered pressure much nearer the bottom of the search interval than the top of it.", "The 880.000000 psia of the matching descent, the two together giving the band a delivery point can occupy on a hill of 3000.000000 ft in either direction.", "The ceiling of 784.964657 psia, which is as close to the inlet as any rate can bring the outlet."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Reduce the rate towards nothing and the outlet rises towards 784.964657 psia and stops there. It never reaches 850.000000 psia at any rate, because the gas has to lift itself 3000.000000 ft before delivering anything.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-the-outlet-pressure', 13, 'A target rate beyond the line returns an error saying the line cannot carry it even to an atmospheric outlet. Why would returning the floor of the bracket instead be a particularly dangerous response?', '["It is below the base pressure of 14.650000 psia, so every rate computed downstream of it would be counted at a condition the published forms do not report at.", "It is a valid pressure that would pass any check testing whether a number came back, and it would flow into a sizing sweep as though the line delivered the contracted rate.", "It would be indistinguishable from the answer on a descending line, where an outlet below the inlet is the ordinary case and a low delivered pressure raises no question.", "It would contradict the round trip, since running that pressure forward through the same form returns a rate the caller never asked for."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The solver went to the bottom of its range, found the line still short, and said so. The distance between that finding and a returned floor is the entire point.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-the-outlet-pressure', 14, 'Put the 3000.000000 ft climb under a near-atmospheric inlet of 15.500000 psia and the engine reports that the static gas column alone spends the inlet pressure. How does that differ from the refusal on a rate the line cannot carry?', '["It is the same finding reported at a different point in the call, the rate message being returned when a target was supplied and the column message when one was not.", "The column message is thrown while the rate message is returned, which is how a caller tells a malformed inlet from a duty the line cannot meet.", "The ceiling has fallen to or below the floor, so there is no interval left to search at all, whereas the rate refusal is the solver searching a real interval and finding the line short at the bottom of it.", "The column message reports that the rate requested was too large for the inlet given, so lowering the target recovers an answer in the way it does for the other refusal."]'::jsonb, 2, 'That message is about the column alone, before any rate has been considered and before any friction has been spent. The range itself has closed.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-the-outlet-pressure', 15, 'A designer wants to know in advance whether a target outlet is answerable on a climbing line. What does the ceiling let them decide before a solve is attempted?', '["That a target above the ceiling has no answer inside this method, because the driving group has already vanished up there and no rate can bring the outlet that high.", "That a target above the ceiling will be answered at the ceiling itself, since the solver clamps to the nearest reachable pressure and reports the rate that goes with it.", "That a target above the ceiling requires a different form, because the four published correlations carry different ceilings on the same hill and one of them will contain the target.", "That a target above the ceiling is reachable at a low enough rate, the ceiling being the outlet at the largest rate the line will pass rather than at the smallest."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The ceiling needs the inlet and the elevation group, both of which the engine already returns. Knowing it in advance turns a refusal from a surprise into a confirmation.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-marching-a-profile', 1, 'The OGBIA traverse reports four stations over 26400.000000 ft, the first of them at 0.000000 ft and 900.000000 psia. What is different about that first row?', '["It is the only station the engine places itself, the remaining three being read off the segment list the caller supplied.", "It is a result like the others, computed by running the first segment backwards from the arrival pressure of 874.339369 psia so that the whole list is internally consistent.", "It is an input rather than a result, because a traverse answers where the line is and that question needs to be told where the line started.", "It is a gauge pressure, the inlet being the one figure a traverse takes relative to atmosphere before converting the rest of the list to absolute."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A single pressure-drop call answers what the line spends. Without an inlet there is no pressure at station 0 and nothing to subtract from.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-marching-a-profile', 2, 'The OGBIA line is marched flat and then over a ridge that stands 420.000000 ft at its two middle stations. Both arrive at 874.339369 psia. What separates the two cases?', '["At station 1 the ridge stands at 732.488123 psia against 891.446456 psia on the flat line, a difference of 158.958333 psi that no arrival pressure reports.", "Nothing measurable, since the ridge climbs and descends the same 420.000000 ft and a profile that returns to its starting elevation is equivalent to a flat one at every station.", "The spend, which is 25.660631 psi on the flat line and larger on the ridge by the static column the gas had to lift over the crest.", "The number of stations, the ridge case adding two rows at 420.000000 ft that the flat case does not carry."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A line is sized on its worst station and not on its last one. The crest is where the pressure is lowest and it is the station a review has to see.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-marching-a-profile', 3, 'Why does the ridge cost nothing over the whole line?', '["Because the two middle stations sit at the same 420.000000 ft, so the climb and the descent fall inside the first and last segments and cancel by construction, which is why the same ridge costs a gas line nothing either.", "Elevation is a static column, and on an incompressible liquid a column is paid for on the way up and returned on the way down, so the total spend is the friction alone at 25.660631 psi.", "Because the engine nets the profile to its total elevation change before marching it, and the ridge nets to 0.000000 ft between the two ends of the line.", "Because the friction over the ridge is lower than over the flat line by exactly the static column, the two effects offsetting to leave the same arrival."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The profile climbs 420.000000 ft and descends the same distance. That is a property of a liquid line, and the gas half of this tier does not have it.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-marching-a-profile', 4, 'The ridge returns all of its elevation to an incompressible liquid. What does the gas half of this tier do with the same shape of profile?', '["It returns slightly more than it took, because a descending gas line recovers more head than the matching climb spent and the two together leave the rate above where it started.", "It returns it as well, since the elevation term in a gas form is built from the same static column and a column is symmetric whatever fluid stands in it.", "It refuses the profile, because a gas call carries a single elevation change and an undulating route cannot be expressed as one figure.", "It does not return it, because the gas elevation term sits inside an exponential and equal distances up and down do not cancel there."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A liquid line adds its elevation term and hands it back at the far side, which is why both marched cases arrive at 874.339369 psia. A gas line scales the outlet inside the driving group and moves the length the friction acts over at the same time, so a profile leaves a residue.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-marching-a-profile', 5, 'The traverse spends 25.660631 psi over 26400.000000 ft in three segments and a single call over the same length spends 25.660631 psi. What follows from a difference of 0.000000 psi?', '["That the station list is the entire value of the call, so a reader is choosing between a number and a profile rather than between a rougher answer and a better one.", "That the traverse is redundant on this line, and worth running only where the segments differ enough in slope for the accumulated answer to separate from the single call.", "That three segments are too few to resolve the difference, and a survey with a station every few hundred feet would show the marched total pulling away from the single call.", "That the single call is internally marching the same three segments, which is why the two agree exactly and why only one of them needs to be run."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A liquid is incompressible, so the density, the velocity, the Reynolds number and the friction factor do not change along the pipe. Splitting it changes nothing the arithmetic depends on.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-marching-a-profile', 6, 'The digest prints the difference between the marched and single-call totals as 0.000000 psi rather than as a small number. Why is an exact zero worth more than a small residual?', '["A small residual would breach the six-decimal convention the liquid half of this course prints at, and a figure that cannot be printed at its stated precision cannot be checked against a golden.", "A small residual would mean the traverse had dropped a boundary between two of its three segments, which is the one defect a total can diagnose on its own.", "A small residual would need explaining, and the explanation would be either a segmentation effect or an arithmetic one with no way to tell them apart from the total alone.", "A small residual would place the answer outside the tolerance the published cases are checked at, so the comparison could not be used as a check on the segment loop."]'::jsonb, 2, 'An exact agreement between two differently assembled paths is a test the digest performs rather than asserts. A mishandled accumulation or a dropped boundary would show as a difference.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-marching-a-profile', 7, 'What would have to be present for a marched total to differ from a single call over the same length?', '["A steeper profile, since the elevation term is what accumulates differently between a march and a single call and a flat or returning profile hides that difference.", "Something that makes a segment''s answer depend on which segment it is, such as a changing density, a changing bore or a changing duty at an offtake.", "A larger number of segments, because each boundary introduces a rounding step and the marched total drifts from the single call in proportion to how finely the line was cut.", "A resistance sum on the traverse, since fittings are the one term that accumulates per segment rather than per line."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A changing density is what makes a compressible fluid a different problem. None of the three is present on this line, so the agreement is exact rather than close.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-marching-a-profile', 8, 'A single call on the OGBIA line carrying the isometric''s 4.500000 velocity heads spends 25.793983 psi while the traverse spends 25.660631 psi. What is the gap of 0.133351 psi?', '["A segmentation effect, the three segments each resolving their own velocity head and the single call resolving one for the whole length, which is why the marched figure is the lower of the two.", "The elevation term, which the single call carries over the full 26400.000000 ft and the traverse distributes across its three segments at a slightly different total.", "An accumulation of rounding across the three segment boundaries, small on this line and growing with the number of stations a caller asks for.", "Exactly the fittings, because the traverse has no resistance-sum argument at all."]'::jsonb, 3, 'There is nowhere to put a fitting list on a traverse, so the fittings are absent by construction rather than approximated.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-marching-a-profile', 9, 'Why is the traverse dropping the fittings described as an omission in the signature rather than an error in the arithmetic?', '["Because a missing argument is a property of the call that is visible before the call is made, by looking at what it accepts, while an arithmetic defect is something that might be fixed in a release.", "Because the arithmetic inside each segment is correct only for a line with no fittings, so the defect is in the domain the traverse is valid over rather than in the code that implements it.", "Because the traverse reports a zero fittings field on every station, which records the omission in the output and makes it a declared limit rather than a mistake.", "Because the gap of 0.133351 psi is small beside 25.660631 psi, and a difference of that size is a modelling choice rather than an error in either call."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The traverse is not wrong. It computed a correct answer to the question it can be asked, and it cannot be asked about fittings.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-marching-a-profile', 10, 'On this line the fittings are worth 0.133351 psi against 25.660631 psi of pipe. Why is the size of that gap not the lesson?', '["Because the gap scales with the length of the line, so the same isometric over a longer route would give a proportionally smaller fraction and the omission would matter less.", "Because the two calls answer the same question and the gap is therefore a measure of the accuracy of the march rather than of anything the march leaves out.", "Because the gap is exactly whatever the fitting list is worth, so a line with the same pipe and a heavier fitting count has the same structural omission and a larger consequence.", "Because the traverse prints a note whenever the omission is large enough to affect a total, so a reader is warned on the lines where the size of the gap does matter."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Nothing in the traverse output says any of this. There is no zero fittings field and no note, so the absence is silent.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-marching-a-profile', 11, 'Push 20000.000000 bpd of 56.000000 lb/ft3 crude at 8.000000 cp through 2.067000 in of bore over 20000.000000 ft, entering at 100.000000 psia. What does the traverse do?', '["It returns the station list as far as the pressure stayed positive and reports the remaining stations as null, leaving the caller to decide how far the line is usable.", "It refuses, reporting that the line cannot carry this rate because the pressure reaches zero absolute before the end of the line.", "It returns the stations with a negative pressure at the last of them, since a traverse reports what the arithmetic produced and leaves the physical reading to the caller.", "It clamps the arriving pressure to atmospheric and attaches a warning that the duty exceeds what the line will pass."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A rate that costs more than the inlet holds is a rate the line cannot pass. The traverse is the call that knows the inlet, so it is the one in a position to refuse.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-marching-a-profile', 12, 'The refusal on that line arrives with evidence attached. How many stations does it report standing behind, and what is the last of them?', '["Three stations, the last of them at 20000.000000 ft, which is the distance the refusal names as the point the march died at and therefore the last row it computed.", "No stations at all, since a refused call returns its error in place of the list and the distance it died at is the only positional figure it carries.", "Two stations, the inlet at 100.000000 psia and the point at which the pressure first crossed zero absolute, which together bracket the failure.", "One station, at 0.000000 ft and 100.000000 psia."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Four pieces of evidence travel with the error string: the stations it stood behind, the last of those stations, the distance it died at of 20000.000000 ft, and the pressure the arithmetic produced there.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-marching-a-profile', 13, 'The refusal reports that the arithmetic produced -47328.563502 psia at 20000.000000 ft. Why is that figure shown rather than returned?', '["Because it is not a pressure at all, so it is presented as evidence of what the arithmetic implied rather than as an answer to the question that was asked.", "Because it is the drop rather than the pressure, and a drop is reported separately from the station list on every traverse whether or not the call refuses.", "Because it lies below the floor of the bracket the traverse searches, so it can be quoted as a bound on the answer but not as the answer itself.", "Because it is an intermediate value from the segment loop that the engine had not yet corrected for elevation when the refusal fired."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine ran the case, produced arithmetic, examined what that arithmetic implied and declined to present the result as a pressure. A call that had not checked would have returned it as though it were an answer.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-marching-a-profile', 14, 'The same line that the traverse refuses is answered by the single call underneath, which reports a spend of 47428.563502 psi. Why do the two calls disagree?', '["The single call omits the fittings while the traverse carries them, and on a duty this severe that difference is large enough to move one call across the physical boundary and not the other.", "The single call works in the friction term alone while the traverse adds the elevation, and the line falls far enough over 20000.000000 ft for the two to separate by that margin.", "They were asked different questions: the single call was asked what the duty costs, and the traverse was asked where the fluid is starting from 100.000000 psia.", "The single call converged and the traverse did not, which is why one of the two returns a number and the other returns the last state its iteration reached."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A line can cost more pressure than any inlet holds, in the same way a journey can cost more money than is in the account. The cost is real and the balance is what makes it impossible.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-marching-a-profile', 15, 'Of the evidence the refusal carries on that failing line, which piece is the actionable one for a designer?', '["The pressure the arithmetic produced, because its size against the inlet says how far the duty would have to fall before the line could carry it.", "The distance it died at, because compared against the length of the line it says whether the duty is slightly beyond the line or nowhere near it.", "The error string, because it names the state the method has no answer for and therefore says which of the stated inputs has to be changed.", "The number of stations the march stood behind, because it locates the failure along the profile in the same way a worst station does on a line that completes."]'::jsonb, 1, 'That figure is the difference between resizing a bore and rethinking a scheme. The error string says only that the line cannot carry the rate, which is what the designer already suspected.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 1, 'A reviewer is handed one line for the SOKU trunk. Which reading carries the whole of what this tier can say about it?', '["11.938000 in over 32.000000 miles carrying 66104956.1404 scfd, which is the Weymouth rate and therefore the conservative figure a reviewer should work from.", "11.938000 in over 32.000000 miles from 850.000000 to 620.000000 psia, a driving group of 338100.0000 psia squared, four rates from 66104956.1404 to 88369202.2673 scfd with General Flow at a friction factor of 0.0112132010, and 666.307057 psia delivered at 60000000.0000 scfd.", "A driving group of 338100.0000 psia squared and a spread of 1.336801 across the four forms, the individual rates being recoverable from those two figures and the ratio column.", "11.938000 in over 32.000000 miles at an average flowing temperature of 535.000000 degR and an average compressibility of 0.885000, the eight conditions being the reading and the rates following from them."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Four forms and one line, with a spread of 1.336801 across them and a step of bore that moves the answer further still. A rate alone does not say which of the four produced it.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 2, 'The Professional reading carries one figure the engine could not evaluate and had to search a bracket for. Which is it?', '["The friction factor of 0.0112132010, which General Flow searches for inside the same bracket the outlet pressure uses, running from atmospheric up to the ceiling a hill moves.", "The equivalent length factor of 1.0408761444, which has no closed expression and is searched for once s is known.", "The driving group of 338100.0000 psia squared, which the engine resolves by bisection because the two pressures enter it squared and the inverse of a square is not unique.", "The 666.307057 psia the trunk delivers at 60000000.0000 scfd."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine has no closed inversion, so it bisects on the outlet pressure inside a bracket whose ceiling the hill moves. Nothing else on the reading needed a bracket.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 3, 'Put the SOKU trunk up 1500.000000 ft and Weymouth reads 61666790.6494 scfd. Put it down the same distance and it reads 70296168.5930 scfd. What may not be concluded from that pair?', '["That a route climbing 1500.000000 ft and then descending the same distance leaves the rate where it started, because the two do not average back to the flat 66104956.1404 scfd.", "That the descent is the better case, since the higher of the two rates belongs to it and a designer with a choice of route would take the falling one.", "That the two readings stand several million scfd apart on a trunk that is otherwise unchanged.", "That the flat rate of 66104956.1404 scfd stands between the climbing figure and the descending one."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The term is an exponential and an exponential is not symmetric about zero. Averaging the two fractions on that row gives 0.998132, which is close to one and is not one.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 4, 'The ceiling on the flat SOKU trunk is 850.000000 psia, on a 3000.000000 ft descent it is 920.423606 psia and on the matching climb 784.964657 psia. Why do the three differ?', '["Each is the outlet the trunk delivers at on that profile, so the three differ for the same reason the rates differ and the ceiling is a restatement of the answer.", "Each is the inlet corrected by the equivalent length factor of its row, which is why a climb lowers the figure and a descent raises it.", "Each is the inlet divided by the square root of that row''s e to the s, so the hill moves the end of the bracket and only a flat line leaves it at the inlet.", "Each is the pressure at which the line reaches the largest rate it can pass on that profile, which a hill moves because it changes how much the friction has to spend."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A descent puts the ceiling above the inlet and a climb below it. Reading the offset is reading how much of the answer an inlet-capped bracket could not have expressed.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 5, 'A gas rate arrives on a report with no other figure beside it. Which of the conditions behind it can be recovered from the rate alone?', '["The form, since the four rates on any one line are distinct and a rate can therefore be matched back against whichever of the four produced it.", "None of them, because the form, the efficiency, the elevation and the base are all stated rather than discovered and the answer carries no record of any of them.", "The efficiency, because it scales the rate directly and can be divided back out once that same form''s rate at an efficiency of 1.000000 is known.", "The base, since a rate in scfd is by definition counted at 520.000000 degR and 14.650000 psia, and no figure reported in those units could have been counted at any other base."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Every answer on the reading is conditional on something stated. A gas figure travels with its conditions or it does not travel at all.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 6, 'The OGBIA line flat and over a ridge both arrive at 874.339369 psia while standing 158.958333 psi apart at the crest. What does that contribute to the Professional reading?', '["That a march and a single call disagree once a profile has relief on it, the arrival being the figure the two share and the crest the figure that separates them.", "That the elevation on a gas line is returned in full when a profile climbs and descends the same distance, which is what lets a route be netted to one figure.", "That the traverse is the more accurate of the two calls on an undulating line, since it resolves the crest that a single call over the whole length averages away.", "That a line is sized on its worst station and not on its last one."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The arrival is the same and the middle of the line is not. That difference is invisible in every report quoting an arrival pressure.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 7, 'The digest prints gas rates to four decimals and the friction factor General Flow settles on to ten. Why does the tier insist a figure be quoted at the precision the engine printed it?', '["Because rounding on the way through moves every figure computed from the rounded one, and the tolerances a graded gas value is marked against are far tighter than the rounding would be.", "Because the two conventions distinguish a gas quantity from a liquid one, so the number of decimals is how a reader tells which half of the engine a figure came from.", "Because the goldens are checked at those precisions, so a figure quoted at fewer digits cannot be matched against the published case it belongs to.", "Because the grading tolerance is set at the last printed digit, so a rounded figure falls outside the band whatever its physical accuracy."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine chose how many digits to print and the course grades what the engine returned. Quoting at that precision is one of the capstone habits.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 8, 'The transmission efficiency is held for the literature in this course. What does the Professional reading do with it?', '["Omits it, since a held item may not appear in a course''s teaching material and the reading is confined to figures the engine stands behind.", "Fixes it at 1.000000, which is the only value the four forms were fitted at and therefore the only one a reading may quote.", "States it as a condition of every gas figure, since a rate is not reproducible without it and this package defends no particular value.", "Reports it as a range across the published cases, whose efficiencies of 0.920000, 0.950000 and 1.000000 bracket what a real line achieves and are the three values a reading may quote."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The SOKU reading is at an efficiency of 1.000000 and says so. A rate computed at an efficiency the question did not name is a different answer to a different question.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 9, 'A summary reports a descending line''s drop as a positive number. What has that done to the reading?', '["It has lost the elevation change, because the sign of the drop is the only place a traverse records which way the profile ran.", "It has moved the figure outside the bracket, since a positive drop on a descent implies an outlet below the floor the solver searched from.", "Nothing, provided the profile is stated alongside it, because the magnitude is the quantity a summary reports and the direction is read off the survey.", "It has put the terminal below the station on paper when the line in fact arrives higher than it left, and the sign was the answer."]'::jsonb, 3, 'On the 3000.000000 ft descent the inverse returns an outlet of 880.000000 psia against an inlet of 850.000000 psia and a drop of -30.000000 psi. Taking the absolute value is the mistake.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 10, 'What single property of the SOKU trunk is it that obliges a designer to defend the choice of transmission form?', '["The diameter exponent of 2.6666666667, which every one of the four forms carries and which therefore fixes what a change of bore is worth whichever form is chosen.", "The spread of 1.336801 from the lowest of the four rates to the highest on identical inputs.", "The friction factor of 0.0112132010, which is the only description of the pipe any of the four forms carries and therefore the only figure a bore decision can be checked against.", "The driving group of 338100.0000 psia squared, which is fixed by the two pressures and leaves the bore as the only remaining variable in the reading."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A spread of that width across the answers has to be defended. One step of bore from 11.938000 in to 15.000000 in spreads 1.838340 on the same trunk, so the bore moves the answer further still and both choices are defended rather than defaulted.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 11, 'A reviewer wants to check a gas answer before anything else on the sheet. Which figure lets them do it, and why?', '["The friction factor General Flow settled on, because it is the one number on a gas answer that reports what the form concluded about the pipe rather than what it was told.", "The driving group, because a form handed the right two pressures cannot return a wrong rate, so checking the group is checking the answer.", "The ratio against Weymouth, because it removes the efficiency and the base from the comparison and leaves only the difference between the correlations.", "The equivalent length factor, because it is the part of the elevation adjustment a reviewer can check against the survey rather than against the engine, and the only one of the two the engine returns."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A value of 0.0112132010 on a trunk of 11.938000 in is a report about the flow the form settled on. No Weymouth or Panhandle answer offers that.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 12, 'This tier met several refusals and read each as a finding. What does the reading do when the engine refuses?', '["Reports the nearest figure the engine can supply, since a reading with a gap in it cannot be compared against another reading of the same line.", "Reports the refusal together with the value the arithmetic produced, since the engine attaches that figure precisely so it can stand in the answer''s place.", "Reports the refusal, because the gap was the finding and writing a plausible figure into it turns a correct reading into a wrong one.", "Reports a zero, because a line that cannot deliver passes nothing and a total has to be able to add the branch up."]'::jsonb, 2, 'An outlet meeting its inlet, a rate the line cannot carry to atmosphere and a climb the static column alone pays for each return an error string rather than a number.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 13, 'What did this tier change about the method, as against the Associate tier''s liquid line?', '["The precision of it, gas work being printed to four decimals against the liquid half''s six, which is what a reader has to carry across between the two halves.", "The shape of it: pressures enter squared, the base a rate is counted at is part of the answer, the hill acts twice and never symmetrically, and the inverse has a range a hill moves.", "The units of it alone, the gas forms being stated in miles while the liquid correlations are stated in feet and the elevation staying in feet on both.", "The number of answers, four published forms replacing the single closed-form chain the liquid line is assembled from while the arithmetic underneath stays the same."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The compressible half changed the method rather than the numbers. A reader who can say why the outlet of a descending line can stand above its inlet is ready for the next tier.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 14, 'What two questions about a pipe does the Expert tier take up that no pressure drop can answer?', '["What the transition band between the two friction branches should return, and what roughness a fitted curve may be extrapolated past, both of which are limits this tier met and did not resolve.", "What efficiency a real line achieves, and what base a contract written against another operator''s meter is counted at, neither of which this engine can produce.", "What the worst station on a gas line is, and what a gas traverse would report, since the marching function in this engine is a liquid one.", "What wall the pipe needs, and what liquid sits inside it for a pig to push."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Everything in this tier treated the pipe as a bore. A pipe is also a wall a code demands, and a line is also a volume that collects liquid.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 15, 'Which figures on the Professional reading would move if the trunk were re-run at a different transmission efficiency?', '["All four rates and the delivered pressure at a contracted rate, because every one of the four forms carries the efficiency and the inverse solves against the form it was given.", "The four rates alone, since the inverse is solved on the driving group and the efficiency enters after the pressure has been found.", "The four rates and the driving group of 338100.0000 psia squared, since the efficiency enters the group before any of the four forms is evaluated.", "None of them, because an efficiency of 1.000000 is the reference the ratio column of 1.000000, 1.314034, 1.336801 and 1.117335 is built on, and a reading is quoted against that column."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Weymouth moves from 66104956.1404 scfd at 1.000000 to 56189212.7194 scfd at 0.850000. Every graded gas figure in this course states the efficiency it belongs to.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 1, 'A contract sheet carries a gas rate and nothing else. A reviewer wants to reproduce it. What is missing?', '["The bore and the length, which are the only two things a transmission form knows about the pipe and therefore the only two a reproduction needs.", "The friction factor, which is the sole self-description a gas answer carries and can be used to recover whichever of the four forms produced the rate.", "The form, the efficiency, the elevation and the base, none of which a rate records and all of which change the answer.", "The driving group, since the two pressures can be recovered from it and every other condition of a gas form follows from the pressures."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Four forms span 66104956.1404 to 88369202.2673 scfd on one line, the efficiency scales every one of them, and two bases are not comparable. The rate carries no record of any of it.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 2, 'An operator moves the SOKU delivery point from 620.000000 psia up to 845.000000 psia and then to 900.000000 psia, holding the inlet at 850.000000 psia. Describe the three answers.', '["66104956.1404 scfd, then 10466013.4729 scfd, then a refusal naming no flow, because the driving group first shrinks and then goes altogether.", "66104956.1404 scfd, then 10466013.4729 scfd, then 0.0000 scfd, the rate falling smoothly to nothing as the outlet closes on the inlet.", "66104956.1404 scfd, then a refusal at 845.000000 psia because five psi is inside the band the engine declines to resolve, and then the same refusal at 900.000000 psia.", "66104956.1404 scfd, then 10466013.4729 scfd, then a negative rate, since above the inlet the gas flows the other way and the form reports the direction in its sign."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A group of 338100.0000 psia squared at 620.000000 psia has shrunk to almost nothing by 845.000000 psia and is gone by 900.000000 psia. Zero would be a claim the engine declines to make.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 3, 'A junior engineer reports that the SOKU trunk has a pressure drop of 338100.0000 psi. What is the error?', '["That figure is the group at the flat condition, and the trunk''s stated elevation change means the outlet''s square has to be scaled by e to the s before the group is quoted.", "That figure has been computed from gauge pressures, and the two absolute values of 850.000000 psia and 620.000000 psia give a different group entirely.", "That figure is the group for the pair of published pressures at 900.000000 psia and 500.000000 psia rather than for the SOKU trunk''s own two ends.", "That figure is the driving group in psia squared rather than any pressure at all, so it cannot be read as a pressure drop whatever the two ends of the line are."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A liquid habit subtracts the two ends and reads what comes out as a drop. Here the two ends were squared first, so the figure being quoted is measured in psia squared.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 4, 'Everywhere else in this engine a hill changes an answer. Where does a hill change which answers exist at all?', '["At the equivalent length factor, which changes the length friction acts over and so decides how much of the inlet pressure is available to be delivered.", "At the ceiling of the outlet-pressure bracket, which is the inlet over the square root of e to the s and therefore moves with the slope.", "At the driving group, which vanishes on any line whose outlet meets its inlet and therefore removes every answer above that pressure on every profile.", "At the transition between the friction branches, which a hill can push a line across by changing the rate it carries."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The ceiling is the statement that at a given inlet and a given slope there is a pressure the outlet cannot be above, however little gas is moving. A climb of 3000.000000 ft puts it 65.035343 psi under the inlet.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 5, 'A delivery point on a descending line is reported at 880.000000 psia against a station pressure of 850.000000 psia. A reviewer marks it as an error. Are they right?', '["No. On a 3000.000000 ft descent the ceiling is 920.423606 psia, so 880.000000 psia is comfortably inside the range the physics allows and the line carries 29457958.5967 scfd there.", "Yes. An outlet meeting or exceeding an inlet is exactly the state the no-flow refusal covers, so the reported figure cannot have come from this engine.", "Yes, unless the elevation change was entered in miles rather than feet, which is the entry error that produces an outlet above an inlet on a line that in fact climbs.", "No, but only because the figure is inside the printing tolerance of the inlet, and a delivery point above a station pressure is otherwise refused."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The column of gas recovers more head than the friction spends, so outlets above the inlet are real operating points. The inverse returns a drop of -30.000000 psi for that case.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 6, 'Two rows of the same descent are run, one with the outlet at 880.000000 psia and one at 700.000000 psia, and both recover their outlet with an error of 0.000000 psi. What does the second row rule out?', '["The elevation term, since a case whose outlet sits below the inlet carries no static column and therefore isolates the friction treatment from the hill.", "The form, since two outlets on one profile agreeing to 0.000000 psi shows that whichever of the four was named is being computed as published.", "The bisection as a mechanism, since everything shared by the two rows is held fixed between them and only the position of the answer against the interval has moved.", "The bracket, since a row that recovers its outlet exactly proves the answer lay inside whatever interval was searched for both rows."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A weakness in the method would show on both rows at once. Carrying 65259103.7761 scfd, the row with its outlet at 700.000000 psia is the one whose answer was never in doubt.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 7, 'A specification requires the OGBIA line to hold above 800.000000 psia everywhere along it. Marched over the ridge, the line arrives at 874.339369 psia. Is the specification met?', '["Yes, since 874.339369 psia is what the line delivers and a delivery pressure is what a specification is written against.", "It cannot be answered from a traverse, because the engine reports pressures only at the stations the caller supplied and the minimum may fall between two of them.", "Yes, because the ridge gives back all 420.000000 ft that it climbs, so the elevation term over the line as a whole is 0.000000 psi.", "No, because station 1 stands at 732.488123 psia and a requirement written on the whole line is answered where the line is lowest."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The flat case stands at 891.446456 psia at the same distance. An arrival pressure is silent about every station in front of it, which is why the traverse returns the list.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 8, 'Panhandle A returns the second highest rate on the flat SOKU trunk and falls furthest of the four when the trunk is put up 1500.000000 ft. What do those two facts together tell a designer choosing a form for a route with relief on it?', '["That Panhandle A should be avoided on a route with relief, since a form that loses most on a climb is the one least likely to be describing the line correctly.", "That the choice of form settles not only the rate on the flat but also how much of it the survey can take away, since Panhandle A reads 86864172.0167 scfd flat and falls to 0.927767 of that climbing.", "That the two facts are the same fact, because a form returning a higher rate on the flat has more rate available to lose and the fractions follow from the rates.", "That Panhandle A carries a different elevation term from the other three, which is why it responds to the same hill by a different amount."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The elevation term is shared by all four and each combines it with its own friction treatment. Panhandle A also gains most descending at 1.068566, and Weymouth moves least in both directions.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 9, 'A sizing sweep catches the refusal on a rate the line cannot carry and substitutes the largest rate the line will pass. What has been lost?', '["The distinction between what a contract asked for and what the line can do, since a caller who asked what pressure a contracted rate arrives at has not asked what rate is achievable.", "The refusal itself, which is the only record that the solver reached the floor of its bracket and can be recovered from nothing else in the output.", "The bracket, since a substituted rate has no outlet pressure attached and the sweep can no longer say which interval the answer was searched over.", "Nothing of substance, provided the substitution is reported, since the largest rate the line will pass is the most useful answer available in that state."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Returning the floor would be worse still: it is a valid pressure that passes any check testing whether a number came back. The solver went to the bottom of its range and found the line short.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 10, 'Two refusals in the outlet-pressure solver look similar on a sheet. One names a rate the line cannot carry to atmosphere and one names a static column that spends the inlet. What separates them?', '["The first concerns the duty and the second the profile, so the first can be cleared by lowering the target and the second by lowering the target as well once the hill is netted.", "The first is returned and the second thrown, which is how a caller distinguishes a duty the line cannot meet from an input the method cannot accept.", "The first is a real interval searched to its floor and found short, and the second is an interval that has closed because the ceiling has fallen to or below the floor.", "The first is raised by the forward form and the second by the inverse, which is why only one of them can appear on an outlet-pressure call."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under a near-atmospheric inlet of 15.500000 psia on a 3000.000000 ft climb, the second message is about the column alone, before any rate has been considered.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 11, 'A review quotes a marched liquid line''s total as the pressure drop of that line. The isometric carries 4.500000 velocity heads. What is wrong with the quoted figure?', '["It overstates the drop by the fittings, since a march accumulates a velocity head at each of its three segment boundaries that a single call over the whole length applies once.", "It omits every elbow, valve and exit on the isometric, because the traverse has no resistance-sum argument and the single call with the fittings spends 25.793983 psi against the traverse''s 25.660631 psi.", "It is correct as a total but cannot be attributed to any station, since the traverse distributes the fittings evenly along the line rather than placing them.", "It mixes two conventions, the traverse reporting an absolute arrival pressure and a pressure drop being a difference, so the two cannot be quoted as the same quantity."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The gap of 0.133351 psi is the fittings and nothing else. The omission is in the signature rather than in the arithmetic, so it is visible before the call is made.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 12, 'At 1500.000000 ft of rise on the SOKU trunk the engine returns 1.0828513009 and 1.0408761444. What is each one for?', '["The first scales the outlet''s square inside the driving group and the second scales the inlet''s square, the two together forming the full adjustment a hill applies to the group.", "The first is the ratio of the climbing rate to the flat rate and the second the ratio of the equivalent length to the laid length, so both are outputs read against the flat case.", "The first scales the whole driving group and the second the gas gravity, which is how the weight of the column enters a form that has no rate yet to work with.", "The first scales the outlet''s square inside the driving group and the second scales the length friction acts over."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Two columns, two jobs. One scales a pressure inside the group and one scales a length outside it, and both collapse to 1.0000000000 at an elevation change of 0.000000 ft.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 13, 'Two contractors report the same trunk, one at 86864172.0167 scfd and one at 88369202.2673 scfd, and each names the form it used. What still has to be agreed before those two figures can be subtracted?', '["The base they are counted at, because a rate in scfd is a rate at a stated base and two figures quoted at different bases are not comparable even when they agree.", "Nothing further, since both forms are named and the published exponents of 2.6182000000 and 2.5300000000 convert either rate into the other.", "The bore, because the two Panhandles were fitted over different diameter ranges and a rate is comparable only inside the range its own form covers.", "The efficiency alone, since it is the only input anywhere in this package with no publication standing behind it, and every other condition a gas answer depends on follows from the form that produced it."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The published base is 520.000000 degR and 14.650000 psia. A rate carries no record of the base it was counted at, and neither of these two carries one either.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 14, 'A solver is written to search the outlet pressure between atmospheric and the inlet on any line it is handed. Which of the SOKU trunk''s five elevations would that solver be unable to express an answer for?', '["The two climbs, whose brackets end at 816.835331 psia and 784.964657 psia, both of them beneath the atmospheric floor the search starts from.", "None of them, since the search runs from atmospheric to the inlet and the outlet of any line that carries gas at all lies between those two.", "The two descents, whose brackets end at 884.511201 psia and 920.423606 psia, both of them above the inlet of 850.000000 psia.", "The flat one alone, where the bracket ends at the inlet of 850.000000 psia itself and the search is left with no interval to work in."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A descent recovers more head than the friction spends, so its bracket ends above the inlet. A search bounded at the inlet truncates those answers rather than refusing them.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 15, 'A review records the ridge as costing the trunk a static head of 1500.000000 ft. What does that description leave out of what the engine does with the figure?', '["Nothing material, since e to the s of 1.0828513009 and the equivalent length factor of 1.0408761444 are two renderings of one static head, and either of them alone reproduces the engine''s answer.", "The equivalent length factor of 1.0408761444, which moves the length the friction acts over and is a second effect entirely, separate from scaling the outlet.", "The direction, since the engine takes the size of the rise and charges it as a cost whichever way the line runs, so a climb and a descent of 1500.000000 ft cost the trunk the same.", "The base pressure of 14.650000 psia, which the elevation group converts the rise against before the driving group of 338100.0000 psia squared is formed."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A hill does two things and they are not the same thing. It scales the outlet inside the driving group through e to the s, and it changes the length the friction acts over.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 16, 'Two operators write contracts in scfd against lines in different climates carrying different gas. What makes the two contracts mean the same thing?', '["Both rates are counted at the flowing conditions of their own line, which is what a standard cubic foot is defined against and why the base is exported for a caller to read.", "Both rates are computed with the same transmission form, which is the condition that has to match before two rates can be set beside one another.", "Both rates are corrected to the compressibility of the gas each line carries, so that a quantity counted on one line is the same quantity when counted on the other.", "A standard cubic foot is an accounting unit fixed at 520.000000 degR and 14.650000 psia, and neither of the two climates reaches inside it to change that."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The flowing temperature and the compressibility belong to the line and change with it. Two forms quoted at different bases are not comparable even when they agree.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 17, 'The Weymouth up and down fractions at 1500.000000 ft average to 0.998132. What practical reading does that support?', '["That an undulating route may be netted to its total elevation change, the residual of that averaging being small enough to disregard on any ordinary profile.", "That the descent is the more expensive half on every form, so a profile with more fall than rise in it is the one to check first.", "That an undulating route has not done nothing to the gas, because a climb and an equal descent do not return the rate to where it started.", "That the engine applies a correction of that size to a flat-line rate before reporting it for a route with relief, which is why the figure is printed."]'::jsonb, 2, 'It is close to one and it is not one. On the Weymouth row the climb loses more than the descent gains, and the engine will answer a netted figure honestly for a line that climbs once.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 18, 'A terminal contracts for 60000000.0000 scfd on the flat SOKU trunk. What does the studio report, and what kind of trust does the figure carry?', '["666.307057 psia at a drop of 183.692943 psi, with the trust of a closed-form result, since the round trip recovers the outlet each form came from with a difference of 0.000000 psi.", "666.307057 psia at a drop of 183.692943 psi, and the trust of a search rather than of an equation, since it is only as good as the bracket it ran in and the form it called.", "666.307057 psia at a drop of 183.692943 psi, with the trust of a published case, since that duty is one of the rows this package carries a golden for.", "A refusal, since 60000000.0000 scfd is below the largest rate the line will pass and the solver only reports a pressure for a target it cannot meet."]'::jsonb, 1, '183.692943 psi is what the terminal pays for its duty, and the pressure behind it came out of a bisection. Where a number came from is part of what a reader has to carry with it.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 19, 'A marched liquid line and a single call over the same length agree to 0.000000 psi. What is the right conclusion?', '["That the march is redundant on any incompressible line, and worth running only where the profile has enough relief for the two totals to separate.", "That the segment count was too low to show the difference, and a finer profile would move the marched total away from the single call.", "That the two calls return the same thing, so a review may quote either of them for either purpose.", "That the station list is the whole value of the march, so there is no accuracy tradeoff to weigh."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Nothing the arithmetic depends on varies from one end of an incompressible line to the other, so cutting it into pieces cannot move the total. What the pieces buy is the station list.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 20, 'On the flat trunk an outlet of 900.000000 psia against an inlet of 850.000000 psia is refused outright. On a descent of -3000.000000 ft an outlet of 880.000000 psia against that same inlet is answered. How do the two rulings stand together?', '["The guard is about the driving group, which vanishes once the outlet meets the inlet on a flat line, while a descent supplies head from the column that the group never sees.", "They do not stand together, and the descent is exactly the case the guard was written for: an outlet standing above its own inlet is refused on every profile once the elevation term is put into the comparison at all.", "The guard belongs to the forward direction and the descent was run through the inverse, which brackets the outlet and therefore never compares it against the inlet at any step of the search.", "The descent is answered because a compressibility of 0.885000 is applied to the static column and not to the flowing gas, which lifts the reported outlet above the inlet as an artefact."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A flat line whose outlet meets its inlet has nothing left to drive it. A descent of 920.423606 psia at the ceiling arrives higher than it left because the column returns more than the friction spends.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 21, 'An engineer treats a ridge on a gas line the way the OGBIA liquid case treats one, expecting the climb and the descent to cancel. What goes wrong?', '["Nothing goes wrong on the arrival, which cancels on both fluids, and the error is confined to the crest pressure that only a march reports.", "The gas form refuses an undulating profile, since a gas call carries a single elevation change and cannot express a ridge at all.", "The gas elevation term sits inside an exponential, so the two do not cancel, while on the incompressible liquid the column is paid on the way up and returned on the way down.", "The gas case cancels more than completely, so the expectation errs in the conservative direction and the arrival is understated rather than overstated."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The OGBIA ridge climbs 420.000000 ft and returns all of it, leaving the friction alone at 25.660631 psi. On the gas side 0.932862 and 1.063402 average to 0.998132.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 22, 'A studio calls a gas form, does not check the returned object for an error property, and passes the result into a total. What happens on a dead line?', '["The total is silently reduced by that branch, since a refused call contributes zero and a zero is indistinguishable from a shut line in a sum.", "An object flows onward where a number was expected, because the refusal is returned rather than thrown and nothing compels the caller to look at it.", "The call throws at the point of use, which is late but recoverable, and is the reason the refusal is returned rather than raised at the call site.", "The engine substitutes the largest rate the branch will pass, which is the behaviour the refusal exists to make visible."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The refusal arrives as an object carrying an error property, and a total that adds objects does not stop to complain. The failure surfaces downstream of the call that produced it.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 23, 'Why is the floor of the outlet-pressure bracket what makes the largest rate a line will pass a decidable question?', '["Because the floor is the base pressure the published forms report at, so a rate computed there is the only one expressed in the same units as the target.", "Because the floor and the ceiling together define the interval, and the largest rate is found by bisecting between them in the same way the outlet is.", "Because the floor is fixed while the ceiling moves with the hill, so the largest rate is the one figure on a gas line that a profile cannot change.", "Because the rate at the floor is the most the line can carry, so any target above it needs an outlet the method will not produce."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The solver can ask a question the forward direction cannot: what is the most this line will pass, with the outlet dumped to atmosphere. A target above that is refused rather than approximated.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 24, 'How was the diameter exponent of each transmission form obtained in this package, and why does the method not need the form''s leading constant?', '["By doubling the bore from 10.000000 in to 20.000000 in and taking the base-two logarithm of the two rates'' ratio, in which any constant standing in front of the form cancels.", "Each form''s published statement was read and its exponent quoted, the two engine rates at 10.000000 in and 20.000000 in being printed alongside as a check that the quoted figure reproduces.", "Each form was fitted across the published cases, whose bores run from 6.065000 in to 16.000000 in, and the exponent is the slope of that fit with the constant as its intercept.", "Each form was run at the SOKU bore of 11.938000 in and the exponent recovered from the ratio against Weymouth, which removes the constant by comparison with a reference form."]'::jsonb, 0, 'What comes back is a property of the correlation and of nothing else, which is why it can be set beside a published statement without either one being taken on trust.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 25, 'A reader applies e to the s to both pressures in the driving group. Why does a sweep across bores fail to expose that?', '["Because the mistake moves each row by a different factor, so the rows stop ranking in order of bore and the sweep is discarded before anyone reads a rate off it.", "Because every single row of the sweep is then wrong by one and the same factor, and the only thing a sweep has to compare a row against is the other rows in it.", "Because the sweep prints the friction factor General Flow settled on, and a factor holding steady down the rows is taken as evidence that the group behind it was formed correctly.", "Because the group is still zero when the pressures meet, so the no-flow refusal fires at the right point and the sweep''s boundary behaviour is unaffected."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Only the outlet is scaled. The error also vanishes on a flat line, where e to the s is exactly 1.0000000000, so a flat test case catches neither it nor the version that scales the inlet.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 26, 'A traverse refuses a liquid line while the single pressure-drop call underneath returns 47428.563502 psi on the same inputs. Are the two answers in conflict?', '["Yes. One of the two has to be wrong, and it is the single call, which has no way to notice that the pressure it reports spending exceeds anything the line holds.", "No, because the single call carries the fittings and the traverse does not, and the gap between them on this line is what puts one across the physical boundary.", "Yes, in the sense that both should refuse, and the single call answering is the fails-open half of the pair.", "No. They were asked different questions, and only the traverse was told that the line enters at 100.000000 psia."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The single call was asked what the duty costs, and a cost can exceed a balance. The traverse was asked where the fluid is, and it is the call that knows the inlet.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 27, 'The trunk is asked for 60000000.0000 scfd through Weymouth and delivers at 666.307057 psia. A colleague reruns the same request through Panhandle B. What may be said about the answer before it is computed?', '["That it will be a different pressure, because the inverse solves against whichever form it was handed and the four disagree by 1.336801 on this line.", "That it will be the same, since the search works on the driving group and the driving group of 338100.0000 psia squared is a property of the two pressures alone.", "That it will be lower, since Panhandle B carries the smallest diameter exponent of the four at 2.5300000000 and a smaller exponent costs more pressure on one bore.", "That it cannot be computed, because the inversion is published for Weymouth alone and the other three have no closed form for a search to work from."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine has no closed inversion and bisects on the outlet, calling the published form at every step. A delivered pressure is an answer about a form as much as it is an answer about a pipe.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 28, 'Two answers on the same trunk are handed over, one computed at an efficiency of 1.000000 and one at 0.850000. What may be said about their ratio before the form is known?', '["That it is 0.850000 exactly, because the efficiency scales the driving group before any of the four forms is evaluated and therefore moves every rate identically.", "That nothing may be said at all, since the efficiency enters after the search on every form and two rates at two efficiencies are two unrelated cases.", "Only that it is near 0.850000, since Weymouth and both Panhandles carry the efficiency as a plain multiplier while General Flow reads 0.847757431221 of its own full rate.", "That it is 0.850000 for General Flow and something else for the other three, General Flow being the only one of the four that carries the multiplier anywhere a reader is able to see it applied."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Weymouth reads 56189212.7194 scfd at 0.850000 against 66104956.1404 at 1.000000. General Flow settles its friction factor at the rate it arrives at, so its own ratio sits under the efficiency rather than on it.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 29, 'A route with real relief on it is to be modelled, and the designer has already chosen a transmission form. What has that choice already decided?', '["Nothing further, since the elevation term is shared by all four forms and applies identically whichever of them was named.", "How sensitive the answer is to the survey, since the four forms respond to the same shared elevation term by different amounts.", "The equivalent length factor, since each form converts the laid length differently and the factor is the part of the adjustment the form owns.", "The ceiling of the outlet bracket, since each form vanishes at a different outlet pressure and the bracket is cut to the form that was chosen."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Each form combines the shared adjustment with its own friction treatment. At 1500.000000 ft the four climb to 0.932862, 0.927767, 0.931566 and 0.931850 of their flat rates.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 30, 'A survey shows a route rising and falling several times, and only its two end elevations reach the gas call. What comes back, and what is the exposure?', '["An honest answer for a line that climbs once, which is not the line that was surveyed, because nothing in the call describes the ground between the two ends.", "A refusal, since the elevation guard compares the rise against the length and a netted profile fails that comparison on any route with relief.", "An answer carrying a warning, since the engine detects a netted profile from the mismatch between the elevation change and the equivalent length factor.", "An answer that is conservative, since netting discards the climbs and the descents together and the exponential asymmetry works in the designer''s favour."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine will accept the net figure and answer it. On the liquid side a ridge of 420.000000 ft genuinely returns all of itself, and that is exactly what the gas side does not do.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 31, 'A reviewer wants to distinguish an implementation problem from a method disagreement on the four forms. What does each look like?', '["A spread between the four wider than the published cases show is an implementation problem, and a spread inside that range is the ordinary disagreement between correlations.", "A form whose rate cannot be reproduced at the stated efficiency is an implementation problem, and one that can is a method disagreement by elimination.", "One form drifting away from its own golden is an implementation problem, and every form sitting on its own golden while the four still return different rates is a method disagreement.", "A form whose measured exponent differs from its published one is an implementation problem, and a matching exponent with a differing rate is a method disagreement."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The two look nothing alike once stated. Each of the four is published here with its own golden on five cases, and the spread of 1.336801 survives that agreement.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 32, 'On a profile where the highest ground sits early in the line, why might the worst station not be under it?', '["Because the traverse reports elevation and pressure in separate columns and the worst station is selected on elevation, which is why the two can disagree.", "Because pressure falls with distance as well as with elevation, so a hill late in a line sits at a lower pressure than the same hill early in it.", "Because a station''s pressure is computed from the segment before it, so the lowest pressure always falls one station after the highest ground.", "Because the elevation is returned in full on any profile that comes back to its starting height, which removes the hill from the pressure reading entirely."]'::jsonb, 1, 'On the OGBIA ridge the lowest pressure and the highest ground coincide, because the line is level apart from one ridge and the duty is uniform. The worst station is the one the march reports.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 33, 'The elevation coefficient is reported as 0.037500000000 and described as measured. What makes that a stronger statement than quoting it from a publication?', '["It is an engine return, obtained by setting the gravity, the absolute temperature and the compressibility to one and dividing the 37.500000 the engine gave at 1000.000000 ft by that distance.", "It is checked against a golden, since the elevation group is one of the published cases this package carries measured values for.", "It is derived from the base conditions of 520.000000 degR and 14.650000 psia, which fixes the field units the coefficient belongs to and therefore its value.", "It is recovered from two rows of the elevation table, the ratio of s at 1500.000000 ft to s at 3000.000000 ft isolating the coefficient from the other three inputs."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Setting three of the four inputs to one leaves the coefficient and the elevation change. A number typed into a teaching file would not be an engine return.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 34, 'The trunk is quoted at an average flowing temperature of 535.000000 degR and then run over a ridge of 1500.000000 ft. What has that single average quietly done to the elevation group as well as to the rate?', '["Nothing, since the elevation group takes the gravity, the rise and the compressibility, and the temperature enters the flow term and stops there.", "Replaced it, because the equivalent length factor of 1.0408761444 is computed at the published base temperature of 520.000000 degR rather than at the flowing average the rate itself was taken at.", "Doubled its effect, since the temperature appears once inside s and once in the flow term and the same average is fed to both of them.", "Fixed it, because s divides by the same one absolute temperature, so a column hot at one end and cold at the other is stood in for by one figure there too."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The coefficient inside s is 0.037500000000 and what divides it is the same 535.000000 degR the rate was computed at. One average serves the whole 32.000000 miles in both places at once.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 35, 'A gas line and a liquid line both have a hill on them. What is fundamentally different about how the two calculations treat it?', '["On the liquid line the hill changes the length friction acts over and on the gas line it changes the pressure, which is why only the gas side carries an equivalent length factor.", "On the liquid line the hill is entered in feet and on the gas line in miles, matching the unit each half states its length in.", "On the liquid line the column is a static head paid on the way up and returned on the way down, and on the gas line it is a multiplier inside an exponential that does not return.", "On the liquid line the hill is one of three losses reported separately and on the gas line it is folded into the friction, so it cannot be separated from the rate."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Both halves take the elevation change in feet. The OGBIA ridge returns its 420.000000 ft in full, and the gas fractions of 0.932862 and 1.063402 do not average to one.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 36, 'What does a designer gain by working out the ceiling before attempting a solve rather than after?', '["The bracket is narrowed, so the bisection converges in fewer passes and the answer is returned at a higher precision than the default search allows.", "A target above the ceiling on a climb is known in advance to have no answer, which turns a refusal from a surprise into a confirmation.", "The ceiling can be compared against the inlet to decide which of the four forms to name, since each carries its own ceiling on the same hill.", "The ceiling gives the largest rate the line will pass, which is the figure a contract should be checked against before any pressure is requested."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Nothing new has to be computed for it. On a 3000.000000 ft climb it stands at 784.964657 psia, and a target above that figure can be ruled out on paper.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 37, 'A sheet carries a gas rate of 66104956.1404 scfd and a liquid traverse arrival of 874.339369 psia. What units and precisions do those two belong to?', '["The rate is scfd counted at 520.000000 degR and 14.650000 psia and printed to four decimals, and the pressure is psia printed to six.", "Both are printed to six decimals, which is the convention for any quantity carrying a pressure unit and for the rate because it derives from two pressures.", "The rate is scfd counted at the line''s own flowing conditions and printed to four decimals, and the pressure is psig printed to six.", "The rate is scfd printed to four decimals and the pressure is psia printed to four, counts being the only whole numbers in either half of the engine."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Gas rates and Reynolds numbers print to four decimals, and pressures, ratios and friction factors to six. A gas length is in miles and an elevation change in feet on the same call.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 38, 'A rate is quoted for the SOKU trunk and the form that produced it is not named. How much of the answer has been left unstated?', '["Nothing of the rate itself, since the four agree to within the tolerance the published cases are checked at, and only the friction factor of 0.0112132010 has been lost.", "The equivalent length factor alone, since the four forms share the elevation term and part company only over how each converts the laid length.", "The base the rate is counted at, since each of the four reports at a base of its own and naming the form is what fixes which of them applies.", "A third of it, because Weymouth and Panhandle B answer these same eight conditions with a ratio of 1.336801 between them, and the form was never named."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Weymouth reads 66104956.1404 scfd on this trunk and Panhandle B reads 88369202.2673 scfd. An unnamed form leaves a reader holding a rate that could be either.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 39, 'On a climb a bracket capped at the inlet covers a band above the ceiling, and on a descent it stops short of the answers standing above the inlet. Why is the climbing band the harder of the two to notice on a sheet?', '["Because a climb is the rarer of the two profiles, so fewer lines ever reach that band and a case that arises seldom is a case nobody reports.", "Because the climbing ceiling stands above the inlet, so the band lies outside the bracket altogether and cannot appear in an answer at all.", "Because that band runs from 784.964657 psia up to 850.000000 psia, which are ordinary delivery pressures to look at, while a figure standing above the station it was fed from is odd at a glance.", "Because the climbing band is the narrower of the two, 65.035343 psi against 70.423606 psi, and the smaller of a pair is the one a reviewer passes over."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A climb of 3000.000000 ft puts the ceiling at 784.964657 psia, below the inlet of 850.000000 psia. The matching descent puts it at 920.423606 psia, above the inlet, which is a pressure no inlet-capped bracket could have reached.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 40, 'What has to be true of a figure before this course will grade it?', '["That it is one of the published cases carrying a golden, since a figure with no measured counterpart cannot be marked.", "That it does not rest on a held item, so every graded gas value states its own efficiency rather than assuming one.", "That it is printed to six decimals, which is the precision the grading tolerance is set against for every quantity in the course.", "That it comes from the forward direction, since a figure produced by a search carries the uncertainty of its bracket and cannot be marked exactly."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Every one of the four forms carries the efficiency and no publication in this package stands behind any particular value of it. It is taught as a limit and never graded.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 41, 'Reading across the whole tier, which single change to the method is the one the rest of it follows from?', '["The pressures enter squared, so the quantity that moves along the pipe is a driving group rather than a difference.", "The gas forms state their length in miles, which is what makes a gas call carry two units of length and what the base conditions were fitted against.", "Four published forms replace one closed-form chain, and the squared group is one of the four ways those forms happen to express a difference of pressure.", "The elevation term moved out of a static head and into an exponential, and the driving group is what that exponential multiplies once the hill has been applied."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The density the friction sees is proportional to the pressure, which is what puts the pressures inside a square. The base, the exponential hill and the bracketed inverse are all consequences of it.'),
  ('linesizing', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 42, 'The Expert tier takes up the wall and the pig. What do those two have in common that this tier''s subject does not?', '["Both are computed from the bore and the length alone, which are the two quantities a transmission form already carries, so the Expert tier adds no new inputs.", "Both are governed by published correlations with their own efficiencies, which is the same held-item problem this tier met in the transmission forms.", "Both are answered by the same marching function this tier used on the OGBIA line, applied along the wall thickness and along the liquid holdup in turn.", "Neither can be sized from a pressure drop."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A code demands the wall from the design pressure, the diameter, the grade and where the line runs. A pigging estimate needs one input this engine never computes.');
